.TH CLRUCACHE_SET_FULL 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_set_full \- store a value by key (write-through)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clrucache_set_full(clru_cache cache,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Pairs key_pair with val_pair in the cache.

When the caller gave a remote setter at construction, the library
calls it synchronously, outside the lock of the segment, before it
updates the cache. When the setter fails, this function returns
ccol_unexpected_failure and the cache does not change for that key:
the existing value stays, and so does the exact position of that key
in the eviction order of its segment. A set that stored nothing
therefore gets no protection from eviction, and it cannot cost
another key its place as the next entry to evict.

The set takes effect when the remote setter returns. While the setter
runs, the key keeps its old value, its place in the eviction order and
its place in clrucache_size(). A concurrent insert of another key that
needs room evicts the least recently used entry, which can be the key
of the set, and the eviction callback then receives the old value. If
the setter then fails, the key stays evicted; if it succeeds, the key
comes back with the new value as the most recently used entry of its
segment, which can evict another key. The outcome is always that of
the insert followed by the set, and a segment never holds more entries
than its capacity.

The library serialises many setters for the same key, and a
concurrent getter for the same key blocks until the set completes.
When the set fails and the key is then absent from the cache, that
getter continues as a cache miss, so it runs the remote getter when
the cache has one.

When the cache is at its capacity and the key is new, the library
first evicts the least recently used live entry. A set that succeeds
for a key that the cache already holds makes that key the most
recently used entry of its segment.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache     Cache handle.
key_pair  The key to store. The ptr and size fields must be set.
val_pair  The value to pair with the key. The ptr and size fields must
          be set, and size must be > 0.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success             The function stored the value, and the remote
                         setter succeeded, if the caller gave one.
ccol_unexpected_failure  The remote setter returned false. The set
                         changed nothing, but a concurrent insert can
                         have evicted the key (see above).
ccol_invalid_args        cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                         stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                         key_pair is NULL, or val_pair is NULL, or
                         val_pair->size is 0, or key_pair->size does
                         not agree with the width of the key type of
                         the cache.
ccol_not_enough_memory   The allocation of memory for the new value
                         failed.
ccol_container_full      The internal bookkeeping of the cache reached
                         its maximum element count while the function
                         stored a completely new key. A real program
                         cannot reach this.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle, which the library resolves through a
slot table that it owns before it touches the cache object behind the
handle. That resolve takes a pin, which this call holds for its whole
duration, including any time that the call blocks inside a remote
setter callback, so a concurrent clru_destroy() on the same handle
waits for this call to finish instead of freeing the cache under it.
The average-case complexity of the hash map operations is O(1).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_set (3),
